Below is a compact Java example that shows three common ways to create and run threads.  
The program launches two simple worker threads (one as a `Thread` subclass, one as a `Runnable`), and then it also uses an `ExecutorService` to run another task. All three threads print their progress with a small delay so that you can see that they execute concurrently.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit;

/**
 * A small demo that shows how to create and run multiple threads in Java.
 * 1. MyThread – extends Thread
 * 2. MyRunnable – implements Runnable
 * 3. ExecutorService – runs a task from a thread pool
 */
public class MultithreadingDemo {

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* 1. A thread defined by subclassing Thread */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    static class MyThread extends Thread {
        private final int id;

        MyThread(int id) {
            this.id = id;
            // Give the thread a meaningful name
            setName("MyThread-" + id);
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            try {
                for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                    System.out.printf("%s: count %d%n", getName(), i);
                    Thread.sleep(300);          // simulate some work
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); // restore interrupt status
                System.out.printf("%s interrupted%n", getName());
            }
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* 2. A thread defined by implementing Runnable */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    static class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
        private final int id;

        MyRunnable(int id) {
            this.id = id;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            Thread current = Thread.currentThread();
            current.setName("MyRunnable-" + id);
            try {
                for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                    System.out.printf("%s: count %d%n", current.getName(), i);
                    Thread.sleep(400);          // simulate some work
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                current.interrupt();
                System.out.printf("%s interrupted%n", current.getName());
            }
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* 3. A simple task run by an ExecutorService (thread pool)          */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    static class SimpleTask implements Runnable {
        private final int id;

        SimpleTask(int id) {
            this.id = id;
        }

        @Override
        public void run() {
            Thread current = Thread.currentThread();
            current.setName("ExecutorTask-" + id);
            try {
                for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
                    System.out.printf("%s: count %d%n", current.getName(), i);
                    Thread.sleep(500);          // simulate some work
                }
            } catch (InterruptedException e) {
                current.interrupt();
                System.out.printf("%s interrupted%n", current.getName());
            }
        }
    }

    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    /* Main method – starts all three kinds of threads and waits for them   */
    /* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Main thread starting...");

        // 1. Start a thread by subclassing Thread
        MyThread thread1 = new MyThread(1);
        thread1.start();

        // 2. Start a thread using a Runnable
        Thread thread2 = new Thread(new MyRunnable(2));
        thread2.start();

        // 3. Start a thread via an ExecutorService
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor();
        executor.submit(new SimpleTask(3));

        // Wait for all threads to finish
        try {
            thread1.join();
            thread2.join();
            // Shutdown executor and wait for its tasks to finish
            executor.shutdown();
            executor.awaitTermination(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            System.err.println("Main thread interrupted while waiting for child threads.");
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }

        System.out.println("Main thread finished.");
    }
}
```

### How it works

1. **`MyThread`**  
   * Extends `Thread`.  
   * Each instance prints 5 messages with a 300‑ms pause in between.

2. **`MyRunnable`**  
   * Implements `Runnable`.  
   * The same logic is executed when the `Runnable` is wrapped in a `Thread` object.

3. **`SimpleTask` + `ExecutorService`**  
   * Demonstrates how to use a thread pool (`ExecutorService`) to execute a task.  
   * In this example a single‑thread pool is used, but any pool size will work.

The `main` method starts all three threads, waits for them to finish (`join` for the explicit threads, `awaitTermination` for the pool), and then exits. When you run the program you’ll see the three threads interleaving their output, proving that they truly run in parallel (subject to the OS’s scheduling).
